On the region $x>0$, $y>0$, consider

$$
(x^2y+y^3)\,\mathrm{d}x-x^3\,\mathrm{d}y=0.
$$

Is $I(x,y)=\dfrac{1}{xy^2}$ an integrating factor? Complete the test. After multiplying by $I$, write the equation as $M\,\mathrm{d}x+N\,\mathrm{d}y=0$:

$M=$ {{field-1}}

$N=$ {{field-2}}

Now compute the cross partials:

$M_y=$ {{field-3}}

$N_x=$ {{field-4}}

An integrating factor requires $M_y=N_x$ throughout the region. Therefore, this candidate {{field-5}} an integrating factor.

